{"description":"A content-addressed store for what the graph cannot hold inline — session transcripts, mermaid diagrams, HTML mockups, user-supplied drawings and photographs, and the source documents a corpus was extracted from. The graph records a CONTENT HASH; the bytes live in the store; an unresolvable pointer fails loud and names itself rather than rendering a design quietly missing its pictures.

HAND-ROLLED AND SYNCHRONOUS, IN `reflow2-core` (`dec:content-store-implementation`, Anthony 2026-07-31). Three operations — `put(bytes) -> hash`, `get(hash) -> bytes`, `exists(hash)` — over `sha2` (already a core dependency) and `std::fs`, with write-to-temp-then-rename for atomicity. Zero new crates, no async runtime, deterministic and testable in memory like the rest of the core. `object_store` was measured and recorded as the documented UPGRADE PATH rather than adopted: 36 crates new to reflow2, ~24 of them URL/ICU machinery a local store never calls, and its value — paths, prefixes, listing, multipart, conditional writes — is exactly the feature set immutable content addressing does not have.

Blobs are COMMITTED to the repo, so they travel exactly as the design does: clone and you have everything, with no second channel to configure or be down (`dec:where-content-lives`). Reading INTO stored content is not this capability's job and never becomes one — the agent navigates, the graph records an opaque locator it never parses (`dec:agent-navigates-content`). PLANNED.","is_entry_point":false,"is_exit_point":false,"name":"Store bytes beside the design and point at them by content hash","provenance":"authored","status":"realized","tier":"operational"}
